    "title": "Austin Drummond claims he was FBI informant before quadruple murder - NewsBreak",
    "text": "( NewsNation ) — Austin Drummond seems to be doing everything possible to try and prove his innocence, including claiming that he was “acting as an FBI informant.” Drummond, who was charged in the quadruple murder of a family in West Tennessee earlier this month, spoke with a reporter from jail and said the FBI recruited him to go undercover after an earlier stint in prison. What was he doing, exactly? Drummond says he was helping bust drug dealers who were sending shipments into the prisons. In addition, Drummond stated that the four victims — James Wilson, Adrianna Williams, Cortney Rose and Braydon Williams — weren’t killed until his cover was blown, which forced him to run for his life. The 28-year-old had previously been linked to the victims, some even being very closely connected. After the murders, an infant who was related to the victims was found in front of a house in Dyer County. The baby was safe and healthy, authorities said. Drummond refused to answer questions about the baby. Drummond admitted he was “directly involved” with the killings, but says he is innocent of the murders themselves. Danny Goodman Jr, the Lake County District Attorney, isn’t buying it. He continues to say Drummond is the guy behind the killings, so much so that they plan to seek the death penalty against him . Goodman Jr. says he knows Drummond’s motive for the killings, but isn’t releasing it yet. Drummond had been dating a state corrections officer who is related to the victims and is pregnant with Drummond’s baby. The corrections officer was reportedly fired from her job over the affair. Drummond is set for a preliminary hearing next month. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to NewsNation.",
